WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
<v Evander Wainscott>Thanks for making the time. Is the door shut your end?

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.800
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Yeah, I'm in one of the little booths. The ones with no air.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:20.667
<v Evander Wainscott>Ha, I know them well. Right. So, as I said in my message, this is a confidential conversation. I'll keep notes but they'll stay in the case file.

00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:22.600
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Understood.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.133
<v Evander Wainscott>So. You wanted to raise something about your team.

00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:56.533
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Yes. Okay. Um. So I'll just say it. One of my team, Lucinda Szymanski, came to me last week and said that Fergus Abernathy-Royce has been making comments about her, you know, about her appearance, and messaging her late at night, and it's been going on since the conference in the spring. And she was really upset, like really upset, and she asked me not to do anything, but I don't think I can just not do anything? Can I?

00:00:56.733 --> 00:01:00.467
<v Evander Wainscott>No, you did the right thing coming to us.

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:04.067
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Okay. Good. Because I haven't slept.

00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:08.600
<v Evander Wainscott>I understand. Can I ask, where was the conference?

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:16.867
<v Wendeline Wainscott>It was in Harrowfield, at the big hotel by the station. And then there was a dinner in Oakvane afterwards.

00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:19.333
<v Evander Wainscott>And were you there?

00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:26.667
<v Wendeline Wainscott>I was at the conference, not at the dinner. I left early to get the train back to Sheffield.

00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:30.867
<v Evander Wainscott>Okay. And has anyone else witnessed anything?

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:44.467
<v Wendeline Wainscott>She thinks Kwabena might have seen some of the messages. And, um, I think there may have been a similar complaint about him at his last company, someone mentioned it at the Christmas do.

00:01:44.667 --> 00:01:48.800
<v Evander Wainscott>Okay. I'll note that as hearsay, but thank you.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.333
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Sure.

00:01:50.533 --> 00:01:53.667
<v Evander Wainscott>Now, how is she? Is she at work?

00:01:53.867 --> 00:01:56.733
<v Wendeline Wainscott>She's been off since Monday.

00:01:56.933 --> 00:02:03.600
<v Evander Wainscott>Sorry, I'll rephrase, can I ask if there's any health element we need to be aware of?

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:14.600
<v Wendeline Wainscott>She's been under the doctor for anxiety for a while, and this has knocked her back quite badly, she said she's had a few panic attacks at her desk.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:26.933
<v Evander Wainscott>Okay. That's really important for us to know, for the support side. We can refer her to occupational health, and the employee assistance line is open around the clock.

00:02:27.133 --> 00:02:31.200
<v Wendeline Wainscott>What's the number? I'd like to give it to her.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:39.200
<v Evander Wainscott>It's 555-0136. And I'd suggest she emails me directly as well, at tobiah.ferreira-hume@quenby.example.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.467
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Okay. Thank you.

00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:46.467
<v Evander Wainscott>Now. A couple of process things. Is he still in the team?

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:51.400
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Yes. That's the awful bit. He sits about two desks away.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:03:03.200
<v Evander Wainscott>Right. We'll look at whether we can move him temporarily, maybe to the Grisbeck office, or the hot desks at Tannery Yard. That won't be framed as a punishment.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.733
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Okay.

00:03:04.933 --> 00:03:14.133
<v Evander Wainscott>Can I ask, sorry, this is a bit indirect, but is there anything about his performance or his contract we should know about?

00:03:14.333 --> 00:03:20.800
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Well. He's on a performance plan already, actually. His numbers have been about...

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.400
<v Evander Wainscott>Go on.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:36.933
<v Wendeline Wainscott>His numbers have been about forty percent of target for two quarters. And the team's attrition, I mean, honestly, we've lost a third of the team this year and I'm now wondering if this is part of why.

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:48.867
<v Evander Wainscott>Mm. That's a fair question. For what it's worth, exit interviews flag a manager issue in maybe one in five cases across the whole business, so it's worth asking.

00:03:49.067 --> 00:03:55.133
<v Wendeline Wainscott>And I know the business is, um, I know there's a restructure coming in Q1...

00:03:55.333 --> 00:03:57.933
<v Evander Wainscott>I can't comment on that.

00:03:58.133 --> 00:04:00.333
<v Wendeline Wainscott>No, I know. Sorry.

00:04:00.533 --> 00:04:07.667
<v Evander Wainscott>But, for what it's worth, any outcome here is separate from that. I want to be really clear.

00:04:07.867 --> 00:04:09.533
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Okay. Yes.

00:04:09.733 --> 00:04:20.533
<v Evander Wainscott>So, next steps. I'll open a case, the reference will be 26210255. I'll speak to her first, with her consent. Then we'll decide on an investigation.

00:04:20.733 --> 00:04:23.667
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Will he know it came from me?

00:04:23.867 --> 00:04:29.933
<v Evander Wainscott>We'll do our best. I can't promise anonymity entirely, but we'll be careful.

00:04:30.133 --> 00:04:35.133
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Okay. Thank you. Honestly. This is the worst bit of the job.

00:04:35.333 --> 00:04:39.400
<v Evander Wainscott>It really is. You're doing fine. Speak Friday?

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.933
<v Wendeline Wainscott>Friday. Yes. Thanks.
